A||||13||HYPTNOTiSE||decided on a new type of v-6 header||||14||
Z||000000||HYPTNOTiSE||06-28-2000||07:14 PM||||Steve Hamm is making me a set of headers, they are constructed with 1.5" primary tubes 16 gauge, 3/8" machined steel flanges, uses 2.5" collectors into a 2 or 2.250" crossover pipe exiting near the stock location.  The tubes are almost all equal length as well. 600 bucks, but i beats leaky manifolds, and with the extent that my engine is built and the amount of nitrous i'm going to be spraying these babys will sure help out high rpm. Plus travis' header results are not really conclusive for my application.  Sure you lose toque below 4000rpm, but do you really need power there while racing, you only dip below 4 grand once when taking off, and the lower torque may even help traction some.  But you can't see the results after 6K rpm either, but you can see that in that range the power starts to increase dramitically. Steve's headers are even nicer than the west coast fiero headers, so i think that power increases should be even higher. We'll soon see.<P>Mike LeCompte||||reg||14
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||06-28-2000||10:55 PM||||if your intent is racing, as in non-drag, and you've built you're engine up very tuff and very light, I'd say go for it. But as for street driving, it'd be a nightmare, and for drag, well, you'd have to launch at such a high RPM that you better get some really lose tires or be prepaired to rebuild the bttom end axles to flexplate. Yes, headers do make you lose some low end torque, exactly how much is beyond my ability. I'm a big fan of Torque tho, so, my opinion is a bit bias, but the only engine I'd ever put nitris on would be a Straight-6, hit the shifts at about 9 grand and leave the competition behind.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Megafiero||06-28-2000||11:06 PM||||If you are going to race an auto-cross and will be running the higher rpm's  sounds fine, otherwise I'd agree with ThatRickGuy.  Torque is a must if you want a car to have a good launch,  all the hourse power you can posible make is useless unless you got alot of torque.  Go with a build that gives you both   I think you will end up happier.  well  whatever you decide,good luck!<P>[This message has been edited by Megafiero (edited 06-28-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Megafiero (edited 06-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||06-28-2000||11:47 PM||||At the Center For Automotive Research at OSU here in Columbus, the students build race cars as class projects (boy, did *I* pick the wrong major!!) But they build these one cylinder racers from the ground up, and they use ALOT of carbon fiber. I watched a guy build a header out of it once. He molded his header out of wax, got the shape and design he wanted, then wrapped the wax with the carbon fiber. After it set up, he heated the pipe, the wax melted out, and viola, a brand new carbon fiber header! I've been thinking that would be the way to make a set for our little machines.....||||reg||3
Z||000004||HYPTNOTiSE||06-29-2000||01:19 AM||||yeah i though about how bad ass an all carbon fiber exhuast would be, but then i remembered i dont know how do use it and i dont have the money to even think about it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||06-29-2000||08:23 AM||||I do MUCH more street racing and drag racing than SOLO events... I'm sure with a better cam or bigger motor these number would be totaly different... Are you close to a dyno? dyno your car now and after the new header fabrication/install!   ||||reg||1
Z||000006||HYPTNOTiSE||06-29-2000||01:55 PM||||I would love to dyno the results of these headers, and i'm going to dyno my car after it's finished, but i'm building my new engine and i sold my ported stock exhauast manifolds for 200 already so theres no way i could do a before and after.  Steves headers are supposed to by much nicer than chris wests, but 600 vs 500.  We'll see, steves hamms headers tubes are almost all the same length.<P>Mike LeCompte<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaun41178||06-30-2000||05:18 PM||||ok some of you guys overlooked a very important thing that hyptnotise said in his original post.  He said he is going to be running nitrous.  Many of you say that he will have a lack of torque and that it won't do as well on the street.  Well from my understanding all V6 Fieros can squeel the tires/spin them when launching off the line even without dropping the clutch at like 3k rpm or higher.  It can do it from idle.  Hyptnotise also said that he has a built up engine too.  He might not have given specifics but trust me it is one tough cookie.  Lots of high po stuff.  His engine will make more torque just off idle then the stock 2.8 v6's did peak.  So he won't have a problem with lack of low end torque that could hurt him on the street.  But even on a stock 2.8 a loss of like 5 or so lb-ft of torque from these headers will not be noticed on the street.  Its too little of a loss to be noticed.<P>Also Hyptnotise is running the bottle like he said above.  So any loss of low end torque again is made up for by his 3.2 built to the hilt engine plus a 70-100 shot of nitrous on that.  Nitrous adds a good amount of hp but holy mother does it add torque.  So if he got frisky he could launch his car off idle on the nitrous.  Torque problems?  No freaking way man!!  With nitrous I would expect over 300 torque at the wheels on his ride.<P>Now headers are a  must with his engine as he will be flowing a lot more air at the higher rpm then a stock 2.8.  hp increases are seen on a stock 2.8 with headers so it is saying that the stock log manifolds are restrictive.  With a built 3.2 and nitrous the stockers would totally choke the top end bringing down torque as well as hp.  If the torque stays high then so does the hp as afterall hp is a factor of torque<P>Headers necessary on  stock 2.8?  Not really as the stock ones can be ported enough to help out.  I can say that I portd my stockers and had before and after done of this mod on a dyno and I lost torque from it.  So for those not wanting to lose torque on the street, don't port your stock manifolds as you will lose about 5-7 lb-ft of torque.  I did gain 8 hp though.  I sacrificed torque for hp but since I am running nitrous too I wanted the best flow possible.<P>Headers on anything larger then the 2.8 like a 3.2 or 3.4, with a larger cam and such, headers are a good idea.  Some low end torque will be lost but when driving you are only in that low range for first gear.  With headers mid and upper range torque will be increased as the engine can breath better and with that torque in the upper and mid range rpm will go up and when torque goes up in that range so does hp.<P>thats it from me for now.<P>Shaun||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||06-30-2000||06:00 PM||||I'm sorry, I wasn't listening, could you repeat that from "OK"....?<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000009||BBBAD GT||07-01-2000||12:21 AM||||<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>So if he got frisky he could launch his car off idle on the nitrous. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ummmmm I wouldn't recommend spraying right off idle.  At that slow of piston speed, and the amount of N2O that fills the chamber, you would bend a rod or something serious.  Maybe a 20 hit wouldn't be that bad for a V6, but I think everybody is talkin 70-100 shot with Fieros.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Sillhouette.com||07-01-2000||12:41 AM||||Yeah don't introduce NOS unless your engine is turning considerably.  With a higher compression engine no matter how bullet proof you make it something will give.<P>Cheers||||reg||1
Z||000011||Shaun41178||07-01-2000||03:07 AM||||Yes you are correct spraying at too low an rpm is dangerous according to many companies and testimonials.  NOS and others say not to do it.  Yes it can be done and bad consequesnces can occur.<P>However I have done this on my 2.8 engine for almos 2 years now.  I have not bent a rod or broken anything.  In fact that is how I use it at the track and on the street.  This is with a stock internally 2.8 as well.  Nothing special about the engine I am using my nitrous on.  Oh yea I am using a 70 shot too.  Basically what I do is power brake it up to 1800 and hit the nitrous and put it to the floor all in one motion.<P>But as I said before many say not to do it cause it will blow something up or bend something or what not.  However personally I have had no problems doing it this way.  Many also say that adding nitrous and one will immediately blow the engine up the first time.  Don't believe everythign that you are told is 100% accurate.  Take somethings with a grain of salt of go by personal testimonials like mine.<P>Warning:  your results with nitrous may vary.<P>Later<BR>Shaun||||reg||1
Z||000012||HYPTNOTiSE||07-01-2000||08:31 AM||||Ugh, i dont think i'm going to bend Small block chevy rods, by spraying off idle. And i think my custom forged venolia pistons can handle it too.  Oh and then cranks been reinforced with mainstuds. a 70 shot isn't jack, i can't wait till i have some miles on the motor, i get my plate system worked up, get my nitrous setup perfected with a progressive controller, so i can slap it to a dyno and throw a 150 hp shot to it, to see what kind of numbers i can turn.<P>------------------<BR>Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ nitrous||||reg||1
Z||000013||BBBAD GT||07-02-2000||12:11 AM||||Shaun:  Do you purge the line right before you hit the N2O off idle?  How long is your N2O line from bottle to intake? If you don't purge, and have a relatively long line from the bottle to the intake, then your liquid is turning into a gas (most likely) when its just sitting in the line, and it's 'hit' isn't as great.  Do you spin the tires at all with this off idle N2O hit?  Just curious.<BR>||||reg||1
